{
  "answer": "Physical cores are the actual hardware components within the CPU, while logical processors (or logical cores) are virtual cores created via hyperthreading technology. Hyperthreading allows a single physical core to execute multiple threads simultaneously, making the operating system see two logical processors per physical core. For example, a quad-core processor with hyperthreading appears as eight logical processors. The key difference is that physical cores are real, independent execution units, whereas logical processors are simulated to improve parallel processing efficiency.",
  "citations": ["1", "2", "3", "6", "8"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "Physical cores are actual hardware components; logical cores are the number of physical cores times the number of threads that can run on each core through hyperthreading, allowing a single core to execute multiple independent threads simultaneously. For example, a 4-core processor with 2 threads per core has 8 logical processors.",
  "citations": ["1", "2", "3", "4"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

