{
  "answer": "Link-local addresses (fe80::/10) are confined to a single link and cannot be routed across routers. They require knowledge of the outgoing interface for communication. Unique local addresses (ULA, fc00::/7) are routable within a site or cooperating sites but not on the global Internet. ULA addresses in the fd00::/8 range are designed to be probabilistically unique, reducing collision risk when interconnecting private networks. Unlike link-local, ULA addresses are treated like global addresses by applications and do not require interface specification.",
