{
  "answer": "iBGP and OSPF are used together to combine the scalability and policy control of BGP with the fast convergence and internal routing capabilities of an IGP. OSPF provides reachability to loopback interfaces, allowing iBGP peers to stay up even when physical interfaces fail, and handles internal routes efficiently. iBGP is necessary for carrying large numbers of external routes (e.g., full Internet routing table) and for applying advanced routing policies (e.g., communities, local preference) that IGPs cannot support.",
  "citations": ["1", "2", "7", "9"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

