{
  "answer": "Qt and GTK are both GUI toolkits used for developing graphical interfaces. Qt is used by KDE, while GTK is the standard toolkit for GNOME. From a user's perspective, the difference is primarily cosmetic, though they set themes independently. Licensing differs: GTK uses LGPL and allows use in proprietary software without fees, whereas Qt's licensing historically restricted free software use and required commercial licenses for proprietary use. Qt is written in C++ and is more complex, requiring a preprocessing step (moc) and often resulting in larger binaries. GTK is written in C and is generally lighter. Additionally, Qt has better cross-platform support but can be heavier on memory usage. Using both toolkits on the same system may consume extra memory (50-100 MB).",
  "citations": ["1", "2", "3", "4", "6", "7"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "Qt and GTK are both GUI toolkits, but they differ primarily in their associated desktop environments (Qt for KDE, GTK for GNOME), licensing (GTK uses LGPL allowing proprietary use without fees, while Qt has more restrictive licensing historically, though now LGPL too), and memory usage (using both may cost an extra 50-100 MB). From a user perspective, differences are mostly cosmetic, but GTK is more common on GNU/Linux, while Qt is preferred under KDE.",
  "citations": ["1", "2", "3"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "GTK and Qt are both GUI toolkits, but they differ in several ways. GTK is the standard for GNOME, while Qt is used by KDE. From a user perspective, the difference is mainly cosmetic (looks) [1][2]. Licensing: GTK uses LGPL, allowing free use even in proprietary software, while Qt has both open-source (GPL) and commercial licenses, which historically limited its use in free software [3][5]. Cross-platform support: Qt is more extensively supported across platforms [3]. Memory: On systems with very limited RAM, using both toolkits can cost an extra 50-100 MB [2]. Programming: Qt is C++-based and requires a meta-object compiler (moc), making it more heavyweight, while GTK is C-based and uses gtkrc files for theming [4][6][7].",
  "citations": ["1", "2", "3", "4", "5", "6", "7"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "GTK is the standard toolkit for GNOME, while Qt is used by KDE. From a user perspective, the main difference is cosmetic (looks). Licensing differs: GTK, as part of the GNU Project, uses LGPL allowing free use in proprietary software; Qt has a more restrictive licensing history, being freely available for non-profit but requiring a commercial license for some uses. Qt is a C++ framework, whereas GTK is a C toolkit. Additionally, different versions (GTK 2/3, Qt 3/4) are not backward compatible.",
  "citations": ["1", "2", "97", "64", "15", "52", "48", "19", "87", "5", "3"],
  "insufficient_context": false
