{
  "answer": "OSPF requires MTUs to match on interfaces forming adjacencies because if the Interface MTU in the Database Description packet is larger than what the receiving router can accept without fragmentation, the packet is rejected. OSPF avoids fragmentation to maintain performance and stability, and mismatched MTUs can cause black-holing of traffic.",
  "citations": ["lotte_technology_search_100k_c623395", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c623396"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "In OSPF, matching MTU is essential because OSPF Database Description packets are rejected if the Interface MTU field indicates a size larger than the receiving interface can accept without fragmentation. This rule prevents fragmentation, which degrades performance and stability. Additionally, mismatched MTUs can cause black-holing of traffic, so OSPF requires valid Layer2 adjacencies including matching MTUs to build reliable forwarding paths.",
  "citations": ["lotte_technology_search_100k_c623396", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c623395"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "MTU must match in OSPF because OSPF rejects Database Description packets that indicate an IP datagram size larger than the receiving interface can accept without fragmentation. This avoids fragmentation, which increases CPU burden and decreases performance, and prevents traffic black-holing due to mismatched Layer2 MTUs. Ensuring MTU match maintains stable and fast protocol convergence.",
  "citations": ["lotte_technology_search_100k_c623395", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c623396"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "MTU must match in OSPF because OSPF rejects Database Description packets that indicate a size larger than the receiving interface can accept without fragmentation, as per RFC 2328. Matching MTUs avoids fragmentation, which increases CPU burden and decreases performance, and prevents black-holing of traffic due to mismatched layer 2 MTUs, ensuring reliable and fast convergence.",
